window.FAQ_INDEX = [{"id": "model-purpose", "title": "What can a price model help us decide?", "theme": "models", "keywords": "forecast trust usefulness validation NPV benchmark", "text": "What can we decide if we cannot predict the exact price? Suppose we buy a share for S 0 = 100 S_0=100 USD/share and plan to sell after T = 1 T=1 year. We want the proceeds to exceed an investment growing at the benchmark rate g y = 0.02 y r − 1 g_y=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . We assume a scheduled sale, no dividends or trading costs, and a constant benchmark rate. Discounting the sale price S T S_T gives the scaled net present value: ρ T = − S 0 + S T e − g y T S 0 . \\rho_T=\\frac{-S_0+S_Te^{-g_yT}}{S_0}. The target ρ T > 0 \\rho_T>0 means that the discounted proceeds exceed the purchase cost. Solving this inequality tells us which future prices count as successes: ρ T > 0 ⇔ S T > S 0 e g y T = 100 e 0.02 ≈ 102.02 U S D / s h a r e . \\rho_T>0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad S_T>S_0e^{g_yT}=100e^{0.02}\\approx102.02\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. Where does the probability come from? Under geometric Brownian motion (GBM), the log price ratio at the sale date is normally distributed. Let μ g = 0.06 y r − 1 \\mu_g=0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} be mean growth and σ = 0.20 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} be volatility. The model writes: ln ( S T / S 0 ) = μ g T + σ T Z , Z ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 1 ) . \\ln(S_T/S_0)=\\mu_gT+\\sigma\\sqrt T\\,Z, \\qquad Z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1). Here Z Z is a standard normal random variable. Substitute the model into the target condition, then subtract the mean and divide by the positive scale σ T \\sigma\\sqrt T : μ g T + σ T Z > g y T , Z > ( g y − μ g ) T σ T = 0.02 − 0.06 0.20 = − 0.20 . \\begin{aligned} \\mu_gT+\\sigma\\sqrt T\\,Z&>g_yT,\\\\ Z&>\\frac{(g_y-\\mu_g)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt T} =\\frac{0.02-0.06}{0.20}=-0.20. \\end{aligned} The normal cumulative distribution function Φ ( z ) \\Phi(z) gives the probability to the left of z z . Our target uses the area to the right, so: ℙ ( ρ T > 0 ) = 1 − Φ ( − 0.20 ) ≈ 0.5793 . \\mathbb P(\\rho_T>0)=1-\\Phi(-0.20)\\approx0.5793. We obtain a 57.9% model probability of beating the benchmark , and a 42.1% probability of finishing at or below it. This supplies a risk comparison for the specified trade. It concerns the sale date; exiting when a price first reaches a boundary is a different trading rule. How much should we trust the answer? If mean growth were 0.02 y r − 1 0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} instead, the threshold for Z Z would be zero and the success probability would be 50%. Estimated inputs therefore matter. The model also assumes constant parameters and independent, normally distributed log-price increments. Clustering or heavier tails in the data can change the risk beyond what a new mean estimate captures. To check the forecast, fit on earlier observations and save probabilities for later, fixed-horizon trades before their outcomes are known. Over many comparable cases, compare the forecast probabilities with the frequency of the same event. For a prediction interval at one date, we check whether the price at that date lies inside it. To check a band intended to contain an entire path, every observed price must remain inside its bounds; one crossing is enough to fail that check. The L5a out-of-sample example develops that distinction. Try it. Keep the parameters above but require a 5% scaled NPV. What changes? Show the answer the price threshold becomes 100 ( 1.05 ) e 0.02 ≈ 107.12 100(1.05)e^{0.02}\\approx107.12 , and the probability falls to about 48.2%. We raise the target before asking the model for its probability. A model gives probabilities for a specified decision. We can translate a required gain into a terminal-price threshold, calculate the probability of exceeding it, and examine how assumptions change that answer.", "url": "questions/model-purpose.html"}, {"id": "model-limits", "title": "Why study models that miss important market behavior?", "theme": "models", "keywords": "limitations heavy tails independent increments out of sample validation", "text": "We use a simple model to connect assumptions with consequences we can calculate and test. A forecast can be a probability distribution over outcomes. It does not have to name the exact future price to be a forecast. The fixed-parameter binomial lattice has only two possible one-step growth rates and independent successive moves. It cannot reproduce heavy-tailed one-step growth or volatility clustering. Constant-parameter GBM permits a continuous range of price movements, but its growth rates are normal and independent, so it also misses those features. Fitting parameters does not make either model memorize the observed path. It summarizes the data within the chosen structure. If later observations disagree with the model, we should examine both parameter uncertainty and structural assumptions. The disagreement alone does not tell us which caused it. A useful next step is to ask a specific question: does the forecast assign reasonable probabilities to later outcomes, under a clearly stated evaluation rule? This is why we separate fitting from out-of-sample testing. Updating parameters may help, but it must be tested on observations unavailable when each forecast was made. A simple model connects assumptions with consequences we can calculate and test. Its forecasts become useful when we state the event clearly and compare issued probabilities with later outcomes.", "url": "questions/model-limits.html"}, {"id": "growth-and-drift", "title": "How do mean growth and drift differ?", "theme": "growth", "keywords": "mu mu_g μ μ_g half variance correction typical median expected average", "text": "Which average do drift and mean growth describe? Geometric Brownian motion uses drift μ \\mu for expected price growth and volatility σ \\sigma for the size of random changes. With constant parameters, the price equation is: d S ( t ) S ( t ) = μ d t + σ d W ( t ) . \\frac{dS(t)}{S(t)}=\\mu\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dW(t). Here W ( t ) W(t) is a Wiener process; its increment over Δ t \\Delta t has variance Δ t \\Delta t . The random price increment has zero conditional mean, so averaging the price equation gives d 𝔼 [ S ( t ) ] / d t = μ 𝔼 [ S ( t ) ] d\\mathbb E[S(t)]/dt=\\mu\\mathbb E[S(t)] . Solving this mean equation gives 𝔼 [ S T ] = S 0 e μ T \\mathbb E[S_T]=S_0e^{\\mu T} . We will compare that expression with the average obtained from the log-price solution to find how drift relates to mean growth. For the price at a fixed time T T , the solution in terms of mean growth μ g \\mu_g is: ln ( S T / S 0 ) = μ g T + σ T Z , Z ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 1 ) . \\ln(S_T/S_0)=\\mu_gT+\\sigma\\sqrt T\\,Z, \\qquad Z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1). Averaging this equation makes the random term vanish because 𝔼 [ Z ] = 0 \\mathbb E[Z]=0 . Thus μ g \\mu_g is expected log growth per unit time. To average the price itself, we exponentiate first. The normal identity 𝔼 [ e a Z ] = e a 2 / 2 \\mathbb E[e^{aZ}]=e^{a^2/2} , valid for any real number a a , gives: 𝔼 [ S T ] = S 0 e μ g T 𝔼 [ e σ T Z ] = S 0 e ( μ g + σ 2 / 2 ) T . \\mathbb E[S_T] =S_0e^{\\mu_gT}\\mathbb E[e^{\\sigma\\sqrt T Z}] =S_0e^{(\\mu_g+\\sigma^2/2)T}. Comparing this result with 𝔼 [ S T ] = S 0 e μ T \\mathbb E[S_T]=S_0e^{\\mu T} gives μ = μ g + σ 2 / 2 \\mu=\\mu_g+\\sigma^2/2 , or μ g = μ − σ 2 / 2 \\mu_g=\\mu-\\sigma^2/2 . This is the half-variance correction . Averaging prices and averaging their logarithms are different operations; the normal exponential average supplies the precise difference for GBM. The L4b derivation obtains the same correction from Itô’s lemma. A numerical comparison. Let S 0 = 100 S_0=100 USD/share, μ = 0.08 y r − 1 \\mu=0.08\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , σ = 0.20 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , and T = 1 T=1 year. Then μ g = 0.06 y r − 1 \\mu_g=0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , giving: 𝔼 [ S T ] = 100 e 0.08 ≈ 108.33 U S D / s h a r e , median ( S T ) = 100 e 0.06 ≈ 106.18 U S D / s h a r e . \\begin{aligned} \\mathbb E[S_T]&=100e^{0.08}\\approx108.33\\ \\mathrm{USD/share},\\\\ \\operatorname{median}(S_T)&=100e^{0.06}\\approx106.18\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. \\end{aligned} The median splits the possible prices into equal lower and upper halves. It follows from the median Z = 0 Z=0 and the increasing exponential function. The right tail pulls the expected price above the median; neither summary identifies a particular future path. Which number belongs in a simulation? The equivalent updates are: S t + Δ t = S t e μ g Δ t + σ Δ t Z = S t e ( μ − σ 2 / 2 ) Δ t + σ Δ t Z . S_{t+\\Delta t}=S_t e^{\\mu_g\\Delta t+\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,Z} =S_t e^{(\\mu-\\sigma^2/2)\\Delta t+\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,Z}. The same draw Z Z gives the same price in both forms. If we estimate μ g \\mu_g from observed growth rates, the correction is already included; subtracting it again would lower the simulated mean growth incorrectly. Try it. Hold μ = 0.08 y r − 1 \\mu=0.08\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} fixed and increase σ \\sigma to 0.40 y r − 1 / 2 0.40\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . Show the answer μ g = 0 \\mu_g=0 , the expected one-year price remains about 108.33 USD/share, and the median becomes 100 USD/share. Higher volatility separates the two averages further. Drift controls the growth of the expected price. Mean growth describes expected log growth per unit time. The half-variance correction connects these two averages under GBM.", "url": "questions/growth-and-drift.html"}, {"id": "growth-return-volatility", "title": "How do growth rate, log return, and volatility differ?", "theme": "growth", "keywords": "sigma σ annualize units 252 sqrt delta t rate return", "text": "We start with the same observed price change and express it in different units. For observations separated by Δ t \\Delta t years, the log return r k r_k and growth rate g k g_k are: r k = ln ( S k S k − 1 ) , g k = r k Δ t . r_k=\\ln\\left(\\frac{S_k}{S_{k-1}}\\right), \\qquad g_k=\\frac{r_k}{\\Delta t}. Quantity Meaning Units when time is in years r k r_k Log return over one observation interval Dimensionless g k g_k Log return per unit time y e a r − 1 \\mathrm{year}^{-1} σ g \\sigma_g Sample standard deviation of the observed growth rates y e a r − 1 \\mathrm{year}^{-1} σ r ( Δ t ) \\sigma_r(\\Delta t) Sample standard deviation of interval log returns Dimensionless σ ̂ \\hat\\sigma Estimated GBM volatility parameter y e a r − 1 / 2 \\mathrm{year}^{-1/2} For a fixed observation interval, scaling every observation scales its standard deviation by the same factor. Therefore: σ r ( Δ t ) = Δ t σ g . \\sigma_r(\\Delta t)=\\Delta t\\,\\sigma_g. Under the constant-parameter GBM model, growth rates have variance σ 2 / Δ t \\sigma^2/\\Delta t . We recover the model’s volatility estimate using: σ ̂ = Δ t σ g = σ r ( Δ t ) Δ t . \\hat\\sigma=\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,\\sigma_g =\\frac{\\sigma_r(\\Delta t)}{\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}. For daily observations with Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 , a growth-rate standard deviation of approximately 3.1749 y e a r − 1 3.1749\\ \\mathrm{year}^{-1} gives σ ̂ ≈ 0.20 y e a r − 1 / 2 \\hat\\sigma\\approx0.20\\ \\mathrm{year}^{-1/2} . The same calculation starts from daily log-return standard deviation 0.012599 0.012599 and multiplies it by 252 \\sqrt{252} . The first conversion is an exact change of units. Using square-root-of-time scaling to forecast risk over a different horizon requires assumptions about dependence and variability. Volatility clustering is a reason to check those assumptions. The sample mean of the g k g_k estimates μ g \\mu_g . Add σ ̂ 2 / 2 \\hat\\sigma^2/2 to recover μ ̂ \\hat\\mu ; do not add half the unscaled variance of the annualized daily growth rates. They describe a price change or its variability on different time scales. Dividing log return by the observation interval gives a growth rate; converting its standard deviation to GBM volatility also uses the time step.", "url": "questions/growth-return-volatility.html"}, {"id": "volatility-purpose", "title": "If volatility cannot predict direction, why is it useful?", "theme": "growth", "keywords": "volatility direction risk clustering useful persistence duration", "text": "It tells us about the range of outcomes we should be prepared to consider. A price distribution with a larger spread gives different probabilities of crossing a loss or profit threshold, even when we cannot determine the sign of the next move. Volatility clustering is the tendency for large movements to occur near other large movements. We observed persistence in growth-rate magnitudes even when signed growth rates had little linear autocorrelation. The conclusion is that recent large movements can inform our expectation of future magnitude. It does not guarantee another large movement, imply alternating gains and losses, or establish a 50/50 chance of an up move. There is no single duration after which every volatility cluster ends. We can inspect the autocorrelation of magnitudes to describe persistence in a dataset and evaluate models that update risk estimates. An autocorrelation plot is not a countdown to the end of the current episode, and the observed pattern alone does not identify its cause. Volatility describes the range of outcomes we should consider. Movement size changes the probability of crossing a loss or profit threshold even when we cannot determine the next direction.", "url": "questions/volatility-purpose.html"}, {"id": "volatility-clustering", "title": "What persists during a volatile period?", "theme": "growth", "keywords": "clustering persistence autocorrelation magnitude signed direction", "text": "What persists when direction is hard to predict? Volatility clustering describes persistence in the size of movements. A turbulent period can contain both gains and losses. We measure direction with signed growth rates and movement size with their absolute values. First convert consecutive prices into growth rates. For asset i i , the observation interval Δ t j \\Delta t_j divides the log price ratio: g j , j − 1 ( i ) = 1 Δ t j ln ( S j ( i ) S j − 1 ( i ) ) . g_{j,j-1}^{(i)}=\\frac{1}{\\Delta t_j}\\ln\\left(\\frac{S_j^{(i)}}{S_{j-1}^{(i)}}\\right). A change from 100 to 101 USD/share over 1 / 252 1/252 year gives a log return ln ( 1.01 ) ≈ 0.00995 \\ln(1.01)\\approx0.00995 and a growth rate 252 ln ( 1.01 ) ≈ 2.507 y r − 1 252\\ln(1.01)\\approx2.507\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The rate is large because we divide a small one-day change by a small fraction of a year. If that same log-price change occurred on each of 252 trading days, the accumulated log change would be 2.507. Here, we use the per-year scale to record one observed day; a forecast requires a model for the days that follow. The L3a lecture figure: daily SPY and AMD growth rates, a shared high-volatility period, and an isolated AMD price jump. What do we see? This is the figure from L3a’s Volatility Clustering section. The shaded March-2020 period contains many large movements of both signs in SPY and AMD. The isolated April-2016 AMD spike is one large jump. Clustering concerns a sequence of large movements, so we distinguish that turbulent period from the isolated spike. The lecture measures persistence using autocorrelation , the linear association between observations separated by a specified number of time steps. Applying it to signed growth rates asks whether positive or negative deviations tend to recur. Applying it to growth magnitudes asks whether large or small movements tend to recur. Positive magnitude autocorrelation across several lags is evidence of persistence in movement size. Why does this matter for a model? A fixed volatility uses the same uncertainty scale after calm and turbulent days. Clustering suggests that recent movement sizes contain information about upcoming risk. When we reach the lattice and GBM models, we can ask whether their assumptions preserve or discard that information. Try it. If signed growth has little autocorrelation but magnitude has persistent positive autocorrelation, can we infer the sign of tomorrow’s change? Show the answer this evidence supports persistence in movement size. It supplies no directional prediction or fixed date when a turbulent episode must end. Volatility clustering concerns persistence in movement size. Large gains and large losses can occur in the same turbulent period, so evidence of clustering can inform risk without determining the next direction.", "url": "questions/volatility-clustering.html"}, {"id": "forecasting-and-pricing", "title": "Why does one tree use two probabilities?", "theme": "lattices", "keywords": "risk neutral real world p q arbitrage replication pricing forecasting", "text": "Why does one tree use two probabilities? Suppose a share costs 100 USD today and will be worth either 120 or 90 USD after one step. The up and down factors are u = 1.20 u=1.20 and d = 0.90 d=0.90 . Keep these prices fixed while we ask two different questions. Forecasting: how often might each outcome occur? Let the estimated real-world up probability be p = 0.65 p=0.65 . Weighting the two prices by their forecast probabilities gives: 𝔼 ℙ [ S 1 ] = 0.65 ( 120 ) + 0.35 ( 90 ) = 109.50 U S D / s h a r e . \\mathbb E_{\\mathbb P}[S_1]=0.65(120)+0.35(90)=109.50\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. This average describes the forecast distribution. Its quality depends on whether the estimated probabilities describe later outcomes adequately. Pricing: what should a specified payoff cost today? Suppose a claim pays H u = 20 H_u=20 USD after an up move and H d = 0 H_d=0 after a down move. Let the risk-free accumulation factor over the same step be R f = 1.02 R_f=1.02 . With frictionless trading, short positions allowed, and borrowing or lending at this rate, no-arbitrage requires the risk-neutral weight q q to satisfy: q u + ( 1 − q ) d = R f ⇒ q = R f − d u − d = 1.02 − 0.90 1.20 − 0.90 = 0.40 . qu+(1-q)d=R_f \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q=\\frac{R_f-d}{u-d}=\\frac{1.02-0.90}{1.20-0.90}=0.40. The condition d < R f < u d<R_f<u gives 0 < q < 1 0<q<1 . We use these weights to average the claim’s payoffs and then discount one step: V 0 = q H u + ( 1 − q ) H d R f = 0.40 ( 20 ) + 0.60 ( 0 ) 1.02 ≈ 7.84 U S D . V_0=\\frac{qH_u+(1-q)H_d}{R_f} =\\frac{0.40(20)+0.60(0)}{1.02}\\approx7.84\\ \\mathrm{USD}. Why is that the price? We can build the claim’s payoff from the share and a loan. The claim’s payoff differs by 20 − 0 = 20 20-0=20 USD between the two states, while the share price differs by 120 − 90 = 30 120-90=30 USD/share. Holding 20 / 30 = 2 / 3 20/30=2/3 of a share therefore reproduces the payoff difference. In the down state those shares are worth 60 USD and the claim pays zero, so we need a loan repayment of 60 USD. Borrowing 60 / 1.02 60/1.02 USD today supplies that repayment after one step. After one step the shares are worth 80 or 60 USD, and the loan repayment is 60 USD. The net payoff is therefore 20 or zero, exactly the claim’s payoff. The initial cost is 100 ( 2 / 3 ) − 60 / 1.02 ≈ 7.84 100(2/3)-60/1.02\\approx7.84 USD. A different claim price would let us trade the claim against this replicating position for an arbitrage profit under the stated assumptions. Thus p p describes forecast frequencies, while q q values a payoff through a trading argument. Risk-neutral pricing does not assert that the stock is risk-free or that 40% of actual moves will be upward. Try it. Change only p p from 0.65 to 0.50. What changes? Show the answer the forecast expected price becomes 105 USD/share. The replicated payoff still costs about 7.84 USD because the two possible share prices and the borrowing rate are unchanged. The real-world probability describes forecast frequencies. The risk-neutral weight prices a payoff through a replication argument. They answer different questions on the same price tree.", "url": "questions/forecasting-and-pricing.html"}, {"id": "calibrate-a-lattice", "title": "How do we construct a lattice from observations?", "theme": "lattices", "keywords": "binomial tree lattice calibration u d p recombining paths nodes", "text": "Our data-driven example estimates the up factor from observed positive movements, the down factor from negative movements, and p p from the frequency of up moves. Suppose four consecutive price factors are: 1.02 , 0.99 , 1.04 , 0.98 . 1.02,\\quad0.99,\\quad1.04,\\quad0.98. The estimates are: u = 1.02 + 1.04 2 = 1.03 , d = 0.99 + 0.98 2 = 0.985 , p = 2 4 = 0.50 . u=\\frac{1.02+1.04}{2}=1.03, \\qquad d=\\frac{0.99+0.98}{2}=0.985, \\qquad p=\\frac{2}{4}=0.50. Starting from S 0 = 100 S_0=100 , a two-step lattice has three terminal states: Up moves Terminal price Probability 2 100 u 2 = 106.09 100u^2=106.09 p 2 = 0.25 p^2=0.25 1 100 u d = 101.455 100ud=101.455 2 p ( 1 − p ) = 0.50 2p(1-p)=0.50 0 100 d 2 = 97.0225 100d^2=97.0225 ( 1 − p ) 2 = 0.25 (1-p)^2=0.25 There are four ordered paths but only three terminal states: up–down and down–up reach the same price. Their probabilities must be added. Recombination follows from u d = d u ud=du ; it does not require d = 1 / u d=1/u . The value p = 0.50 p=0.50 comes from this particular sample, not from a requirement of the model. When the observations are stored as growth rates, recover the price factor with e g k Δ t e^{g_k\\Delta t} . This reverses the earlier logarithm and time scaling. The course estimator averages factors within each sign group; averaging the logarithms and exponentiating would be a different estimator. A zero-change observation joins neither factor group but remains in the denominator when estimating p p . We need at least one up move and one down move to estimate both factors. We estimate up and down price factors from observed movements, and the up probability from their frequency. Ordered paths that reach the same terminal price contribute to that node together.", "url": "questions/calibrate-a-lattice.html"}, {"id": "lattice-targets", "title": "Which lattice prices beat a trading target?", "theme": "lattices", "keywords": "NPV target strict inequality threshold binomial tail bid ask spread", "text": "How do we turn a trading requirement into a probability? First find the sale prices that satisfy the requirement, then add the probabilities of those outcomes. We buy shares now and sell at a scheduled terminal date. Scaled NPV compares discounted sale proceeds with the purchase cost. Which lattice outcomes beat our target? Buying n 0 n_0 shares costs n 0 S 0 n_0S_0 today; their sale brings n 0 S T n_0S_T at time T T . Ignoring dividends and trading costs, discount and divide by the purchase cost: ρ T = − n 0 S 0 + n 0 S T e − g y T n 0 S 0 = S T S 0 e − g y T − 1 . \\rho_T=\\frac{-n_0S_0+n_0S_Te^{-g_yT}}{n_0S_0} =\\frac{S_T}{S_0}e^{-g_yT}-1. Try the two-step tree from L3b. Start at 100 USD/share with u = 1.20 u=1.20 , d = 0.90 d=0.90 , and real-world up probability p = 0.65 p=0.65 . Set the benchmark to zero for this illustration and require ρ T > 0.05 \\rho_T>0.05 . The terminal prices are 81, 108, and 144 USD/share for zero, one, and two up moves. The required price is 100 ( 1.05 ) = 105 100(1.05)=105 , so the middle and upper nodes succeed. Their combined probability is: ℙ ( ρ T > 0.05 ) = 2 ( 0.65 ) ( 0.35 ) + 0.65 2 = 0.8775 . \\mathbb P(\\rho_T>0.05)=2(0.65)(0.35)+0.65^2=0.8775. There are two paths to the middle price but only one to the top price. This is why their probabilities have different counting factors. How does the threshold formula reproduce this calculation? At a terminal lattice node with k k up moves, substitute S T / S 0 = u k d N − k S_T/S_0=u^kd^{N-k} . For a target ρ ⋆ > − 1 \\rho_\\star>-1 and factors u > d > 0 u>d>0 , taking logs of ρ ( k ) > ρ ⋆ \\rho(k)>\\rho_\\star and collecting the terms in k k gives: k ln u + ( N − k ) ln d > ln ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) + g y T , k > τ ( ρ ⋆ ) = ln ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) + g y T − N ln d ln ( u / d ) . \\begin{aligned} k\\ln u+(N-k)\\ln d&>\\ln(1+\\rho_\\star)+g_yT,\\\\ k>\\tau(\\rho_\\star)&=\\frac{\\ln(1+\\rho_\\star)+g_yT-N\\ln d}{\\ln(u/d)}. \\end{aligned} The up count is an integer, so the first strictly successful count is ⌊ τ ⌋ + 1 \\lfloor\\tau\\rfloor+1 . Add the binomial probabilities for that count and all higher counts. If τ = 2 \\tau=2 exactly, success starts at three up moves; two only meets the target. Clipping the first count to [ 0 , N + 1 ] [0,N+1] represents the cases where every node or no node succeeds. This turns a trading requirement into a probability over the model’s terminal outcomes. Here τ = ln ( 1.05 / 0.90 2 ) / ln ( 1.20 / 0.90 ) ≈ 0.902 \\tau=\\ln(1.05/0.90^2)/\\ln(1.20/0.90)\\approx0.902 , so the first successful count is one, as we found by inspecting the prices. Try it. Raise the target to ρ ⋆ = 0.08 \\rho_\\star=0.08 with a strict inequality. Show the answer the 108 USD middle node only meets the target. Only 144 USD succeeds, giving probability 0.65 2 = 0.4225 0.65^2=0.4225 . Where do bid and ask enter? Buying at an ask of 100.10 and immediately selling at a bid of 99.90 loses 0.20 USD/share. For executable trade cash flows, use the entry ask and sale bid; a midpoint-based price model omits that spread cost. First translate the required scaled NPV into a sale-price threshold. Then add the real-world probabilities of terminal nodes whose prices strictly exceed it.", "url": "questions/lattice-targets.html"}, {"id": "correlated-shocks", "title": "How do independent inputs produce correlated movements?", "theme": "dependence", "keywords": "loading matrix Cholesky square root A C covariance shared shocks normal", "text": "How can independent inputs produce correlated asset movements? Give the assets a shared random input. Suppose their volatility parameters are 0.20 and 0.30 y r − 1 / 2 0.30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , with correlation 0.50. The required covariance rate and a loading matrix that produces it are: 𝐂 = [ 0.04 0.03 0.03 0.09 ] y r − 1 , 𝐀 = [ 0.20 0 0.15 0.0675 ] y r − 1 / 2 . \\mathbf C=\\begin{bmatrix}0.04&0.03\\\\0.03&0.09\\end{bmatrix}\\mathrm{yr}^{-1}, \\qquad \\mathbf A=\\begin{bmatrix}0.20&0\\\\0.15&\\sqrt{0.0675}\\end{bmatrix}\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2}. The first diagonal loading is 0.04 = 0.20 \\sqrt{0.04}=0.20 . The shared loading is 0.03 / 0.20 = 0.15 0.03/0.20=0.15 ; the remaining loading is 0.09 − 0.15 2 = 0.0675 ≈ 0.259808 \\sqrt{0.09-0.15^2}=\\sqrt{0.0675}\\approx0.259808 . These choices make 𝐀 𝐀 ⊤ = 𝐂 \\mathbf A\\mathbf A^\\top=\\mathbf C . Let Z 1 Z_1 and Z 2 Z_2 be independent standard normal variables. Write 𝐗 = 𝐀 𝐙 \\mathbf X=\\mathbf A\\mathbf Z for the two noise contributions before time scaling. Expanding the matrix product gives: X 1 = 0.20 Z 1 , X 2 = 0.15 Z 1 + 0.0675 Z 2 . X_1=0.20Z_1,\\qquad X_2=0.15Z_1+\\sqrt{0.0675}\\,Z_2. Both assets use Z 1 Z_1 , which produces their covariance. Independence removes the cross term containing Z 1 Z 2 Z_1Z_2 , and each input has variance one, so: Var ( X 1 ) = 0.20 2 = 0.04 , Var ( X 2 ) = 0.15 2 + 0.0675 = 0.09 , Cov ( X 1 , X 2 ) = 0.20 ( 0.15 ) = 0.03 . \\begin{aligned} \\operatorname{Var}(X_1)&=0.20^2=0.04,\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}(X_2)&=0.15^2+0.0675=0.09,\\\\ \\operatorname{Cov}(X_1,X_2)&=0.20(0.15)=0.03. \\end{aligned} These values have units y r − 1 \\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Dividing the covariance by 0.20 ( 0.30 ) 0.20(0.30) recovers correlation 0.50. One draw illustrates the multiplication: if 𝐙 = ( 1 , − 1 ) ⊤ \\mathbf Z=(1,-1)^\\top , then 𝐗 ≈ ( 0.20 , − 0.109808 ) ⊤ y r − 1 / 2 \\mathbf X\\approx(0.20,-0.109808)^\\top\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . Positive correlation allows individual draws with opposite signs; it describes the joint distribution across draws. How do these numbers enter a price step? For asset i i , add mean log growth and the time-scaled noise in the exponent: S i ( t + Δ t ) = S i ( t ) exp [ μ g , i Δ t + Δ t X i ] , μ g , i = μ i − C i i / 2 . S_i(t+\\Delta t)=S_i(t)\\exp\\!\\left[\\mu_{g,i}\\Delta t+\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,X_i\\right], \\qquad \\mu_{g,i}=\\mu_i-C_{ii}/2. For Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 yr, starting prices of 100 USD/share, and both mean growth rates 0.06 y r − 1 0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , the draw above gives next prices about 101.29 and 99.33 USD/share. The same vector is shared across assets within the step; we draw a fresh vector at the next step. Why use a loading matrix? For independent standard normal inputs, Cov ( 𝐀 𝐙 ) = 𝐀 𝐀 ⊤ \\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf A\\mathbf Z)=\\mathbf A\\mathbf A^\\top . Multiplying by 𝐂 \\mathbf C itself would produce 𝐂 𝐂 ⊤ \\mathbf C\\mathbf C^\\top , with the wrong covariance and units. Try it. Replace the loading 0.15 by − 0.15 -0.15 . Show the answer both variances stay the same because they use squared loadings. Covariance becomes − 0.03 y r − 1 -0.03\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and correlation becomes − 0.50 -0.50 . The assets share random inputs through a loading matrix. Multiplying independent standard normal draws by a matrix A produces covariance AAᵀ, so the loadings determine how the assets move together.", "url": "questions/correlated-shocks.html"}, {"id": "covariance-from-data", "title": "How do observations become a covariance matrix?", "theme": "dependence", "keywords": "data matrix G center centered sample covariance Sigma time scaling delta t", "text": "How do observations become the covariance matrix? Suppose three aligned observations of two assets’ growth rates form the matrix below. Each row is one date and each column is one asset; every entry has units y r − 1 \\mathrm{yr}^{-1} : 𝐆 = [ 1 2 2 1 3 3 ] , 𝐠 ′ = [ 2 2 ] , 𝐆 ̃ = [ − 1 0 0 − 1 1 1 ] . \\mathbf G=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\\\2&1\\\\3&3\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad \\mathbf g^{\\prime}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\2\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad \\tilde{\\mathbf G}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1&0\\\\0&-1\\\\1&1\\end{bmatrix}. Average each column to obtain its mean, then subtract that mean from every observation in that column. This measures whether the assets move above or below their own means together. Compute one entry at a time. The first asset’s deviations are ( − 1 , 0 , 1 ) (-1,0,1) , so its sample variance is [ ( − 1 ) 2 + 0 2 + 1 2 ] / ( 3 − 1 ) = 1 y r − 2 [(-1)^2+0^2+1^2]/(3-1)=1\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . For the covariance, multiply deviations on matching dates and add: [ ( − 1 ) ( 0 ) + ( 0 ) ( − 1 ) + ( 1 ) ( 1 ) ] / 2 = 0.5 y r − 2 . [(-1)(0)+(0)(-1)+(1)(1)]/2=0.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. The matrix product performs all these calculations at once: 𝚺 ̂ g = 𝐆 ̃ ⊤ 𝐆 ̃ 3 − 1 = 1 2 [ 2 1 1 2 ] = [ 1 0.5 0.5 1 ] y r − 2 . \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g =\\frac{\\tilde{\\mathbf G}^{\\top}\\tilde{\\mathbf G}}{3-1} =\\frac12\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}1&0.5\\\\0.5&1\\end{bmatrix}\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. The two variances are on the diagonal; their covariance appears in both off-diagonal positions. Their standard deviations are both one, so the correlation is 0.5 / ( 1 ⋅ 1 ) = 0.5 0.5/(1\\cdot1)=0.5 . Which time conversion belongs in GBM? Growth rates divide log returns by the observation interval. For daily spacing Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 yr, the model’s covariance rate and the interval log-return covariance are: 𝐂 ̂ = 1 252 [ 1 0.5 0.5 1 ] y r − 1 , 𝚺 ̂ r = 1 252 2 [ 1 0.5 0.5 1 ] . \\hat{\\mathbf C}=\\frac1{252}\\begin{bmatrix}1&0.5\\\\0.5&1\\end{bmatrix}\\mathrm{yr}^{-1},\\qquad \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_r=\\frac1{252^2}\\begin{bmatrix}1&0.5\\\\0.5&1\\end{bmatrix}. The GBM step uses Δ t 𝐂 ̂ \\Delta t\\,\\hat{\\mathbf C} as its log-return covariance, which recovers 𝚺 ̂ r \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_r . This provides a direct check on the time factors. The growth-rate equation gives covariance 𝐂 / Δ t \\mathbf C/\\Delta t , so 𝐂 ̂ = Δ t 𝚺 ̂ g \\hat{\\mathbf C}=\\Delta t\\,\\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g . Try it. Add 5 y r − 1 5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} to every first-asset observation. Show the answer its mean rises from 2 to 7, but its centered deviations remain ( − 1 , 0 , 1 ) (-1,0,1) . Every covariance entry stays the same. Adding a constant changes the level, not the variability about that level. These three observations make the arithmetic visible; a useful empirical estimate requires enough aligned data to describe the assets under study. L6a uses the resulting means and covariance to compare allocations. Center each asset’s growth-rate observations, multiply deviations from matching dates, and average with the sample denominator. The observation interval then connects growth-rate covariance to the covariance needed in a GBM step.", "url": "questions/covariance-from-data.html"}, {"id": "independence", "title": "When do independent single-asset models agree with MA-GBM?", "theme": "dependence", "keywords": "zero correlation independence SA GBM MA GBM joint normal marginal", "text": "Use the same initial prices, drifts, and individual volatilities in both constructions. The independent simulations then agree with the multiple-asset model’s joint distribution when all cross-asset covariances are zero. Each asset has the same individual distribution in either construction even when the cross covariances are nonzero; what changes is how the assets move together. The exact agreement follows because the model uses jointly normal growth-rate shocks. For a jointly normal vector, a diagonal covariance matrix gives independent components. In a small example, very weak correlations can make independent simulation a reasonable approximation. Whether the difference matters depends on the portfolio, horizon, and number of assets; many small cross terms can add up. Zero correlation does not establish independence for arbitrary data. Consider an equally likely variable X ∈ { − 1 , 0 , 1 } X\\in\\{-1,0,1\\} and define Y = X 2 Y=X^2 . Their covariance is zero, but knowing X X determines Y Y exactly. The normal-model assumption supplies the additional structure in GBM. We should also distinguish equality in distribution from identical simulated paths. Two independent runs can follow different paths even when they use exactly the same model. With matching individual parameters, the models agree in their joint distribution when the cross-asset covariances are zero. Joint normality supplies the connection between zero covariance and independence here.", "url": "questions/independence.html"}, {"id": "fitted-log-price", "title": "What does a fitted log-price line predict?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "regression residual fit log price design matrix slope confidence interval", "text": "What does the fitted line predict at one observation? Suppose the fitted intercept is ln ( 100 ) \\ln(100) and the slope is 0.06 y r − 1 0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . At t = 0.5 t=0.5 yr, the fitted log price is ln ( 100 ) + 0.06 ( 0.5 ) \\ln(100)+0.06(0.5) . Exponentiating gives 100 e 0.03 = 103.05 100e^{0.03}=103.05 USD/share. If the observed price is 104 USD/share, the residual is the observed log price minus the fitted log price: r j = ln ( 104 ) − [ ln ( 100 ) + 0.03 ] = 0.00922 . r_j=\\ln(104)-[\\ln(100)+0.03]=0.00922. The residual is dimensionless because we fitted logarithms. In matrix notation, the row [ 1 , 0.5 ] [1,0.5] multiplies the intercept and slope to make this same prediction. L6b will replace elapsed time with market growth as the predictor. What are we fitting, and what does the interval describe? For observation j j , the lecture fits log price as an intercept plus mean growth times elapsed time. Stacking all observations gives: ln S j = ln S 0 + μ g t j + ϵ j ⇔ 𝐲 = 𝐗 ̂ 𝛉 + 𝛜 . \\ln S_j=\\ln S_0+\\mu_gt_j+\\epsilon_j \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf y=\\hat{\\mathbf X}\\boldsymbol\\theta+\\boldsymbol\\epsilon. Each row of the design matrix is [ 1 , t j ] [1,t_j] . Least squares chooses the intercept and slope to minimize the squared residuals. For a fitted coefficient θ ̂ j \\hat\\theta_j , the standard error estimates its uncertainty; a smaller value indicates greater precision. The lecture’s two-sided confidence interval is: θ ̂ j ± t 1 − α / 2 , ν SE ( θ ̂ j ) . \\hat\\theta_j\\ \\pm\\ t_{1-\\alpha/2,\\nu}\\operatorname{SE}(\\hat\\theta_j). This is an interval for a model coefficient. A prediction interval for a future price must also account for future random price changes. The classical coefficient interval assumes independent, constant-variance, normally distributed errors. GBM log-price errors accumulate through time and share earlier shocks, so this simplifying regression-error assumption limits the interval’s interpretation. Fitting a line alone does not validate its uncertainty calculation. Try it. Does the fitted price 103.05 USD identify GBM’s expected price at six months? Show the answer it describes the fitted log-price trend and, under GBM, the median price. If σ = 0.20 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , expected price is 100 e ( 0.06 + 0.20 2 / 2 ) ( 0.5 ) = 104.08 100e^{(0.06+0.20^2/2)(0.5)}=104.08 USD. We need the volatility correction when averaging prices. The fitted line predicts log price at an observation time. Exponentiating gives a fitted price trend; interpreting it as an expected price under GBM requires the volatility correction.", "url": "questions/fitted-log-price.html"}, {"id": "ema-updates", "title": "What changes when another price observation arrives?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "EMA half life lambda λ update recenter mean variance memory", "text": "What should we do with a new price observation? Convert it to a growth rate, update the estimates using only information available now, and use those estimates for the next forecast. An exponential moving average (EMA) controls how much the new observation changes the estimates. What changes when another price arrives? The new growth observation g k g_k differs from the previous mean by the innovation δ k = g k − m k − 1 \\delta_k=g_k-m_{k-1} . We retain a fraction λ \\lambda of the old information and give the new observation weight 1 − λ 1-\\lambda : m k = λ m k − 1 + ( 1 − λ ) g k = m k − 1 + ( 1 − λ ) δ k . m_k=\\lambda m_{k-1}+(1-\\lambda)g_k =m_{k-1}+(1-\\lambda)\\delta_k. For a 21-observation half-life, λ = 2 − 1 / 21 ≈ 0.9675 \\lambda=2^{-1/21}\\approx0.9675 , so the new observation receives about 3.25% of the mean’s weight. Half-life describes estimation memory; T T describes how far ahead we forecast. Why is the variance update different? Variance is measured about the updated mean. Measuring the old observations about this new center adds the squared shift of the mean to the old variance. Combining their contribution with the new observation gives: v k = λ [ v k − 1 + ( m k − 1 − m k ) 2 ] + ( 1 − λ ) ( g k − m k ) 2 . v_k=\\lambda\\left[v_{k-1}+(m_{k-1}-m_k)^2\\right] +(1-\\lambda)(g_k-m_k)^2. The mean update gives m k − m k − 1 = ( 1 − λ ) δ k m_k-m_{k-1}=(1-\\lambda)\\delta_k , and the new observation’s deviation is g k − m k = λ δ k g_k-m_k=\\lambda\\delta_k . Substituting these two deviations and collecting terms yields: v k = λ v k − 1 + λ ( 1 − λ ) 2 δ k 2 + ( 1 − λ ) λ 2 δ k 2 = λ [ v k − 1 + ( 1 − λ ) δ k 2 ] . \\begin{aligned} v_k&=\\lambda v_{k-1}+\\lambda(1-\\lambda)^2\\delta_k^2 +(1-\\lambda)\\lambda^2\\delta_k^2\\\\ &=\\lambda\\left[v_{k-1}+(1-\\lambda)\\delta_k^2\\right]. \\end{aligned} These are exponentially weighted moments; v k v_k has no finite-sample unbiased correction. Initialize m s = μ ̂ g , 0 m_s=\\hat\\mu_{g,0} and v s = σ ̂ 0 2 / Δ t v_s=\\hat\\sigma_0^2/\\Delta t from the training data. The updated growth variance has units y r − 2 \\mathrm{yr}^{-2} ; multiplying by Δ t \\Delta t and taking a square root gives GBM volatility σ ̂ k = v k Δ t \\hat\\sigma_k=\\sqrt{v_k\\Delta t} . Each new forecast uses the updated estimates. In the course example, those estimates remain fixed over that forecast’s horizon, so updating them does not by itself model future volatility clusters within the horizon. Work one update. For easy arithmetic, take λ = 0.9 \\lambda=0.9 , previous mean m k − 1 = 0.10 y r − 1 m_{k-1}=0.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , previous variance v k − 1 = 4 y r − 2 v_{k-1}=4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , and new growth g k = 1.10 y r − 1 g_k=1.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Then δ k = 1.00 y r − 1 \\delta_k=1.00\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and: m k = 0.10 + 0.1 ( 1.00 ) = 0.20 y r − 1 , v k = 0.9 [ 4 + 0.1 ( 1.00 ) 2 ] = 3.69 y r − 2 . m_k=0.10+0.1(1.00)=0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1},\\qquad v_k=0.9[4+0.1(1.00)^2]=3.69\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. The mean rises, while the variance falls because the new observation is close relative to the previous spread. At daily spacing, updated volatility is 3.69 / 252 = 0.1210 y r − 1 / 2 \\sqrt{3.69/252}=0.1210\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . Try it. Use g k = 3.10 y r − 1 g_k=3.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} instead. Show the answer δ k = 3 \\delta_k=3 , m k = 0.40 m_k=0.40 , and v k = 0.9 [ 4 + 0.1 ( 9 ) ] = 4.41 v_k=0.9[4+0.1(9)]=4.41 . A sufficiently large deviation raises the variance. Save each forecast when issued so its later evaluation uses the information available at that time. An exponential moving average updates the growth-rate mean and variance. Its half-life controls the influence of older observations, while the forecast horizon specifies how far ahead the resulting model is used.", "url": "questions/ema-updates.html"}, {"id": "prediction-bands", "title": "Does a 95% band contain 95% of complete price paths?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "95 coverage prediction interval confidence band path whole window out of sample", "text": "No. A pointwise 95% prediction interval describes the model’s price distribution at one specified future date. Staying inside an interval at every date is a different event. For fitted GBM parameters held fixed and elapsed time τ > 0 \\tau>0 , the bounds are: L z ( τ ) = S 0 e μ ̂ g τ − z σ ̂ τ , U z ( τ ) = S 0 e μ ̂ g τ + z σ ̂ τ . L_z(\\tau)=S_0e^{\\hat\\mu_g\\tau-z\\hat\\sigma\\sqrt\\tau}, \\qquad U_z(\\tau)=S_0e^{\\hat\\mu_g\\tau+z\\hat\\sigma\\sqrt\\tau}. The values z = 1 z=1 , 1.96 1.96 , and 2.576 2.576 give approximately 68%, 95%, and 99% pointwise coverage under the fitted model. They come from standard normal quantiles before exponentiation. The price bounds are not symmetric about the expected price because the price distribution is lognormal. An entire path has many opportunities to leave these bounds. Its prices are dependent across dates, so we cannot obtain the whole-path probability by simply multiplying the pointwise percentages. Also, these bands treat the fitted parameters as known; they do not include uncertainty in their estimates. The lattice example uses expectation-plus-or-minus-standard-deviation bands and computes its whole-window benchmark directly. Because the lattice distribution is discrete, the familiar normal percentage is not automatically its exact pointwise coverage either. Keep each example’s band definition and scoring event explicit. A pointwise interval concerns the price at one specified date. Remaining inside a band at every date is a different event, so whole-path coverage requires its own calculation.", "url": "questions/prediction-bands.html"}, {"id": "simulation-uncertainty", "title": "What uncertainty does a Monte Carlo simulation measure?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "Monte Carlo MC standard error SE prediction interval confidence interval simulation paths", "text": "What does a band around simulated wealth describe? The multiple-asset GBM example holds the fitted parameters and initial allocation fixed, simulates correlated prices, and computes wealth on every path. At a given date, the paths give a distribution of possible wealth outcomes under that model. Separate outcome spread from precision of the mean. Average the wealth values across paths, then measure their spread: W ‾ t = 1 n ∑ k = 1 n W t ( k ) , s W ( t ) = 1 n − 1 ∑ k = 1 n ( W t ( k ) − W ‾ t ) 2 . \\bar W_t=\\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^{n}W_t^{(k)},\\qquad s_W(t)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}(W_t^{(k)}-\\bar W_t)^2}. Suppose 2,000 independent paths give a mean of 1,100 USD and a standard deviation of 150 USD at a selected date. The example’s approximate 95% pointwise prediction band is: W ‾ t ± 1.96 s W ( t ) = 1 , 100 ± 294 U S D . \\bar W_t\\pm1.96s_W(t)=1{,}100\\pm294\\ \\mathrm{USD}. It describes the spread of wealth outcomes on that date. Its normal approximation can be imperfect because wealth is skewed. By contrast, the Monte Carlo standard error of the mean is 150 / 2 , 000 = 3.35 150/\\sqrt{2{,}000}=3.35 USD, giving an approximate 95% interval of 1 , 100 ± 6.57 1{,}100\\pm6.57 USD for the model’s expected wealth. Pointwise coverage refers to a single date; whole-path coverage is a different event. Estimate a target probability. Discount each path’s terminal wealth to obtain ρ T ( k ) \\rho_T^{(k)} . Count the paths strictly above ρ ⋆ \\rho_\\star and divide by the number of paths: p ̂ = # { k : ρ T ( k ) > ρ ⋆ } n , SE ( p ̂ ) = p ̂ ( 1 − p ̂ ) n . \\hat p=\\frac{\\#\\{k:\\rho_T^{(k)}>\\rho_\\star\\}}{n},\\qquad \\operatorname{SE}(\\hat p)=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}{n}}. If 1,360 of 2,000 paths exceed the target, p ̂ = 0.68 \\hat p=0.68 and its standard error is 0.0104 0.0104 , about one percentage point. An approximate 95% Monte Carlo interval is 0.68 ± 0.0204 0.68\\pm0.0204 . This quantifies the effect of using finitely many random paths while keeping the model inputs fixed. The simulation first checks that generated growth rates reproduce the fitted means and covariance. The later comparison with observed 2025 wealth asks a separate question: how well those fitted inputs describe a new period. Try it. Increase the number of independent paths from 2,000 to 8,000. What should shrink? Show the answer Monte Carlo standard errors are approximately halved. The estimated spread of individual wealth outcomes remains near 150 USD because the model and allocation have not changed. More paths improve numerical precision; uncertainty in the fitted inputs requires a separate analysis. The spread of simulated wealth describes modeled investment outcomes. The standard error of a simulated mean or probability measures the numerical uncertainty from using finitely many paths.", "url": "questions/simulation-uncertainty.html"}, {"id": "sim-fit", "title": "What do alpha, beta, and residuals tell us?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "SIM single index alpha beta α β regression SVD design matrix R squared", "text": "The single index model (SIM) relates an asset’s growth rate to market growth. The subscript M M means market; the number of portfolio assets is | 𝒫 | \\lvert\\mathcal P\\rvert . The release uses SPY as the market proxy and fits growth rates from aligned trading days. What does an asset’s beta tell us? The single index model separates the fitted market response from the asset-specific residual: g i , t = α i + β i g M , t + ε i , t , β i = Cov ( g i , g M ) Var ( g M ) = ρ i M σ g , i σ g , M . g_{i,t}=\\alpha_i+\\beta_i g_{M,t}+\\varepsilon_{i,t},\\qquad \\beta_i=\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(g_i,g_M)}{\\operatorname{Var}(g_M)} =\\rho_{iM}\\frac{\\sigma_{g,i}}{\\sigma_{g,M}}. Beta includes both correlation and the ratio of growth-rate scales. With correlation 0.60 and asset variability twice the market’s, beta is 1.20. A 0.10 y r − 1 0.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} increase in market growth then raises fitted asset growth by 0.12 y r − 1 0.12\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Suppose α i = 0.02 y r − 1 \\alpha_i=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and the observed market growth on a day is 0.10 y r − 1 0.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The fitted asset growth is 0.02 + 1.2 ( 0.10 ) = 0.14 y r − 1 0.02+1.2(0.10)=0.14\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . If the observed asset growth is − 0.06 y r − 1 -0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , its fitted residual is − 0.20 y r − 1 -0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The residual records the part of that observation left unexplained by the line. How do we fit the line? Choose alpha and beta to minimize the squared residuals. For a full-column-rank design matrix, the lecture gives: 𝛉 ̂ i = arg min 𝛉 ∥ 𝐲 − 𝐗 ̂ 𝛉 ∥ 2 2 = ( 𝐗 ̂ ⊤ 𝐗 ̂ ) − 1 𝐗 ̂ ⊤ 𝐲 . \\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}_i =\\arg\\min_{\\boldsymbol\\theta}\\lVert\\mathbf y-\\hat{\\mathbf X}\\boldsymbol\\theta\\rVert_2^2 =(\\hat{\\mathbf X}^{\\top}\\hat{\\mathbf X})^{-1}\\hat{\\mathbf X}^{\\top}\\mathbf y. The equivalent sample formulas explain the fitted coefficients: β ̂ i = Cov ̂ ( g i , g M ) s g , M 2 , α ̂ i = g i ′ − β ̂ i g M ′ . \\hat\\beta_i=\\frac{\\widehat{\\operatorname{Cov}}(g_i,g_M)}{s_{g,M}^2},\\qquad \\hat\\alpha_i=g_i^{\\prime}-\\hat\\beta_i g_M^{\\prime}. The intercept ensures that the fitted line passes through the sample means. The SVD example computes the same estimate as 𝐕 𝚺 − 1 𝐔 ⊤ 𝐲 \\mathbf V\\mathbf\\Sigma^{-1}\\mathbf U^{\\top}\\mathbf y . Agreement among the three solves checks the numerical calculation. How much variation did the fit explain? For an asset with nonzero sample variation, compare squared residuals with squared deviations from the asset’s sample mean: R 2 = 1 − ∥ 𝐫 i ∥ 2 2 ∑ t = 1 N ( g i , t − g i ′ ) 2 . R^2=1-\\frac{\\lVert\\mathbf r_i\\rVert_2^2}{\\sum_{t=1}^{N}(g_{i,t}-g_i^{\\prime})^2}. If residuals retain 40% of the original sum of squares, R 2 = 0.60 R^2=0.60 . In this one-predictor regression with an intercept, R 2 R^2 equals squared sample correlation. It measures in-sample fit; later observations are needed to assess forecast usefulness. Try it. Suppose the sample correlation is 0.60 and the asset’s sample standard deviation is twice the market’s. What are β ̂ i \\hat\\beta_i and R 2 R^2 ? Show the answer β ̂ i = 1.20 \\hat\\beta_i=1.20 and R 2 = 0.36 R^2=0.36 . Beta measures sensitivity to market growth, while R 2 R^2 measures the fraction of variation explained. The client interview uses both when screening firms. Reading the design matrix. Each row of 𝐗 ̂ \\hat{\\mathbf X} pairs the number one with that day’s market growth. Multiplying by ( α i , β i ) ⊤ (\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)^{\\top} produces the intercept plus market contribution for each day. The fitted residual vector is what remains after subtracting those fitted values from observations. The estimation example solves the same least-squares problem by normal equations, a QR-based solve, and singular value decomposition (SVD). For the thin SVD 𝐗 ̂ = 𝐔 𝚺 𝐕 ⊤ \\hat{\\mathbf X}=\\mathbf U\\mathbf\\Sigma\\mathbf V^{\\top} , 𝐔 \\mathbf U is N × 2 N\\times2 , and 𝚺 \\mathbf\\Sigma and 𝐕 \\mathbf V are 2 × 2 2\\times2 . Here 𝚺 \\mathbf\\Sigma contains singular values; 𝚺 g \\mathbf\\Sigma_g denotes a growth covariance. Nonzero singular values give full column rank and a unique fit. The single index model separates an asset’s fitted response to market growth from its residual. Alpha is an intercept, beta measures sensitivity to market growth, and a residual records the observation’s unexplained part.", "url": "questions/sim-fit.html"}, {"id": "bootstrap-uncertainty", "title": "How uncertain are the fitted alpha and beta?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "bootstrap standard error coefficient uncertainty confidence intervals parametric gaussian", "text": "How much might alpha and beta change with another dataset? The fitted line gives one alpha and one beta. New observations would move the fitted line. A standard error describes the spread we expect in the fitted coefficient across repeated datasets under our error model; it differs from the day-to-day spread of asset growth. Where does the standard error come from? Conditional on the observed market growth rates, the classical calculation assumes zero-mean errors with constant variance and no correlation across days. With N > 2 N>2 observations and a full-column-rank design matrix, we estimate the error variance after fitting two coefficients: s g , ε , i 2 = ∥ 𝐫 i ∥ 2 2 N − 2 , Cov ̂ ( 𝛉 ̂ i ) = s g , ε , i 2 ( 𝐗 ̂ ⊤ 𝐗 ̂ ) − 1 . s_{g,\\varepsilon,i}^2=\\frac{\\lVert\\mathbf r_i\\rVert_2^2}{N-2},\\qquad \\widehat{\\operatorname{Cov}}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}_i) =s_{g,\\varepsilon,i}^2(\\hat{\\mathbf X}^{\\top}\\hat{\\mathbf X})^{-1}. Square roots of the two diagonal entries give SE ( α ̂ i ) \\operatorname{SE}(\\hat\\alpha_i) and SE ( β ̂ i ) \\operatorname{SE}(\\hat\\beta_i) . Alpha’s standard error has units of inverse years; beta’s is dimensionless. Read an interval. With thousands of observations, the 95% interval is approximately the estimate plus or minus 1.96 standard errors. Suppose α ̂ i = 0.02 y r − 1 \\hat\\alpha_i=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} with standard error 0.03 y r − 1 0.03\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , and β ̂ i = 1.20 \\hat\\beta_i=1.20 with standard error 0.05 0.05 . Then: α i : 0.02 ± 1.96 ( 0.03 ) = [ − 0.0388 , 0.0788 ] y r − 1 , β i : 1.20 ± 1.96 ( 0.05 ) = [ 1.102 , 1.298 ] . \\begin{aligned} \\alpha_i:&\\quad 0.02\\pm1.96(0.03)=[-0.0388,0.0788]\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1},\\\\ \\beta_i:&\\quad 1.20\\pm1.96(0.05)=[1.102,1.298]. \\end{aligned} The alpha interval includes zero, while the beta interval lies above one. Under the regression assumptions, these data estimate market exposure more precisely relative to its magnitude. The estimation example uses the Student- t t critical value; the large-sample Gaussian value is used in the bootstrap comparison. Jointly Gaussian errors under these assumptions give the exact classical t t interval. What does the Gaussian bootstrap hold fixed? The uncertainty example keeps 𝐗 ̂ \\hat{\\mathbf X} fixed, draws independent synthetic residuals from a fitted normal distribution, and adds them to fitted growth: 𝐲 ( k ) = 𝐗 ̂ 𝛉 ̂ i + 𝛆 ( k ) . \\mathbf y^{(k)}=\\hat{\\mathbf X}\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}_i+\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon^{(k)}. For one day, suppose fitted growth is 0.14 y r − 1 0.14\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and a synthetic error draw is − 0.20 y r − 1 -0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . That day’s synthetic observation is − 0.06 y r − 1 -0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Repeat this addition for every day, keeping its market observation, and fit a new line. Repeat the entire process for each of the K = 1 , 000 K=1{,}000 synthetic datasets. The standard deviation of the refitted alphas or betas estimates its standard error. Because these datasets follow the fitted Gaussian model, agreement with the classical calculation checks that calculation. The optional residual-diagnostics example separately examines heavy tails and dependence across days. Try it. Does increasing K K from 1,000 to 4,000 make the original beta estimate twice as precise? Show the answer it improves the numerical precision of the bootstrap comparison. The original observations and their information about beta remain the same. Standard errors describe variation in coefficient estimates across repeated datasets under an error model. A bootstrap constructs and refits synthetic datasets to make that variation visible.", "url": "questions/bootstrap-uncertainty.html"}, {"id": "buy-and-hold-weights", "title": "What stays fixed when we buy and hold?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "weights rebalancing Dirichlet simplex allocations concentration buy hold exact log growth", "text": "What stays fixed when we buy and hold? We choose initial dollar fractions, use them to buy shares, and keep those share counts. The fraction of current wealth in each asset then changes with its price. How do weights become wealth? Allocate the fraction w i w_i of initial wealth W 0 W_0 to asset i i , buying n i = w i W 0 / S i ( 0 ) n_i=w_iW_0/S_i(0) shares. Holding those shares fixed gives: W t = ∑ i n i S i ( t ) = W 0 ∑ i w i S i ( t ) S i ( 0 ) . W_t=\\sum_i n_iS_i(t)=W_0\\sum_iw_i\\frac{S_i(t)}{S_i(0)}. If two equal allocations experience price factors 1.20 and 0.90, the wealth factor is 0.5 ( 1.20 ) + 0.5 ( 0.90 ) = 1.05 0.5(1.20)+0.5(0.90)=1.05 . The first asset now accounts for 0.5 ( 1.20 ) / 1.05 = 57.14 % 0.5(1.20)/1.05=57.14\\% of wealth and the second for 42.86%. Restoring equal fractions would require selling some of the first asset and buying more of the second. Why do portfolio calculations use a weighted growth rate? Over one short interval, substitute the asset price factors e g i Δ t e^{g_i\\Delta t} into the wealth ratio: 1 Δ t ln ( W Δ t / W 0 ) = 1 Δ t ln ( ∑ i w i e g i Δ t ) ≈ ∑ i w i g i = g p . \\frac{1}{\\Delta t}\\ln(W_{\\Delta t}/W_0) =\\frac{1}{\\Delta t}\\ln\\left(\\sum_iw_ie^{g_i\\Delta t}\\right) \\approx\\sum_iw_ig_i=g_p. The approximation uses e x ≈ 1 + x e^x\\approx1+x , ln ( 1 + x ) ≈ x \\ln(1+x)\\approx x , and weights summing to one. It is useful for small interval changes; the wealth formula supplies the exact buy-and-hold calculation. For these price factors, the exact portfolio log return is ln ( 1.05 ) = 0.04879 \\ln(1.05)=0.04879 . The weighted asset log returns give [ ln ( 1.20 ) + ln ( 0.90 ) ] / 2 = 0.03848 [\\ln(1.20)+\\ln(0.90)]/2=0.03848 . The difference shows why we use the exact wealth formula for the investment actually held. What does a Dirichlet draw choose? Dirichlet sampling generates candidate long-only weight vectors. Equal concentration parameters of one give uniform sampling over the simplex, while larger equal concentrations favor weights near equal allocation. Comparing sampled portfolios explores possible allocations; optimization in L6a determines the minimum for the specified constraints. Try it. With two assets and 𝛂 = ( 1 , 1 ) \\boldsymbol\\alpha=(1,1) , must every draw give ( 0.5 , 0.5 ) (0.5,0.5) ? Show the answer no. The distribution samples all splits along the allocation line uniformly; a draw such as ( 0.8 , 0.2 ) (0.8,0.2) is an 80%/20% investment. Concentration controls which allocations we explore, while the price data determine their estimated reward and risk. The initial allocation determines the share counts, which stay fixed in buy-and-hold trading. Current wealth fractions change as prices move, and the exact wealth calculation follows those fixed shares.", "url": "questions/buy-and-hold-weights.html"}, {"id": "portfolio-risk", "title": "How do individual growth rates become portfolio risk?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "portfolio variance diversification covariance correlation sigma risk reward", "text": "How do individual growth rates become portfolio reward and risk? We use fixed weights to form the linear growth proxy g p = 𝐰 ⊤ 𝐠 g_p=\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\mathbf g . Its expected value measures reward, and its variance measures risk. The L6a Dirichlet example compares sampled allocations this way; the data example chooses weights by optimization. Estimate the inputs. Each entry of 𝐆 \\mathbf G comes from an aligned price pair: g i , k = ln [ S i ( t k ) / S i ( t k − 1 ) ] / Δ t g_{i,k}=\\ln[S_i(t_k)/S_i(t_{k-1})]/\\Delta t . Average each column and then average the products of its centered observations with those of another column: g i ′ = 1 N ∑ k = 1 N g i , k , Σ ̂ g , i j = 1 N − 1 ∑ k = 1 N ( g i , k − g i ′ ) ( g j , k − g j ′ ) . g_i^{\\prime}=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{k=1}^{N}g_{i,k},\\qquad \\hat\\Sigma_{g,ij}=\\frac{1}{N-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{N}(g_{i,k}-g_i^{\\prime})(g_{j,k}-g_j^{\\prime}). The diagonal records individual variances. The off-diagonal records whether assets tend to be above or below their own means together. Work through two assets. Suppose the estimated means are ( 0.06 , 0.12 ) y r − 1 (0.06,0.12)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , their growth-rate standard deviations are ( 2 , 4 ) y r − 1 (2,4)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , and their correlation is 0.25 0.25 . For weights ( 0.75 , 0.25 ) (0.75,0.25) , the covariance is 0.25 ( 2 ) ( 4 ) = 2 y r − 2 0.25(2)(4)=2\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . Thus: 𝚺 ̂ g = [ 4 2 2 16 ] y r − 2 , 𝔼 ̂ [ g p ] = 0.75 ( 0.06 ) + 0.25 ( 0.12 ) = 0.075 y r − 1 , Var ̂ ( g p ) = 0.75 2 ( 4 ) + 0.25 2 ( 16 ) + 2 ( 0.75 ) ( 0.25 ) ( 2 ) = 4 y r − 2 . \\begin{aligned} \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g&=\\begin{bmatrix}4&2\\\\2&16\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2},\\\\ \\widehat{\\mathbb E}[g_p]&=0.75(0.06)+0.25(0.12)=0.075\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1},\\\\ \\widehat{\\operatorname{Var}}(g_p)&=0.75^2(4)+0.25^2(16)+2(0.75)(0.25)(2) =4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. \\end{aligned} The portfolio standard deviation is 2 y r − 1 2\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . For Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 yr, its corresponding diffusion-volatility scale is Δ t σ g , p = 0.126 y r − 1 / 2 \\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,\\sigma_{g,p}=0.126\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . The examples use this conversion when comparing growth-rate variability with GBM volatility. Try it. Keep the weights and individual variances fixed, but set the correlation to zero. What changes? Show the answer mean growth stays 0.075 y r − 1 0.075\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , while variance falls to 3.25 y r − 2 3.25\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} and standard deviation to 1.803 y r − 1 1.803\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The smaller covariance reduces portfolio risk. For the course’s linear growth-rate proxy, weights combine asset means and the covariance matrix combines their variances and cross terms. Correlation affects portfolio risk through those cross terms.", "url": "questions/portfolio-risk.html"}, {"id": "minimum-variance", "title": "Why mix assets when one has a smaller variance?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "GMV global minimum variance inverse covariance optimization weights short selling", "text": "Why mix assets when one has a smaller variance? Suppose two uncorrelated assets have growth variances 1 and 4 y r − 2 4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . Put weight w w in the first and 1 − w 1-w in the second. Their portfolio variance is: σ g , p 2 = w 2 + 4 ( 1 − w ) 2 = 5 ( w − 0.8 ) 2 + 0.8 [ y r − 2 ] . \\sigma_{g,p}^2=w^2+4(1-w)^2 =5(w-0.8)^2+0.8\\quad[\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}]. The square is smallest at w = 0.8 w=0.8 , giving variance 0.8 y r − 2 0.8\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , below either asset alone. Each asset’s variance is multiplied by its squared weight, and zero covariance removes the cross term. This calculation finds the best allocation among all weights, whereas random sampling only compares the allocations drawn. How do we generalize to many assets? The global minimum-variance (GMV) problem supplies the covariance matrix and chooses the weights. A positive-definite covariance gives a unique minimum. We first allow signed weights, so the budget is the only constraint: minimize 𝐰 1 2 𝐰 ⊤ 𝚺 g 𝐰 subject to 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 = 1 . \\underset{\\mathbf w}{\\operatorname{minimize}}\\quad \\frac12\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g\\mathbf w \\qquad\\text{subject to}\\quad\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w=1. Why does the inverse covariance appear? We attach a multiplier to the budget constraint and set the gradient to zero: ℒ ( 𝐰 , λ ) = 1 2 𝐰 ⊤ 𝚺 g 𝐰 − λ ( 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 − 1 ) , 𝚺 g 𝐰 = λ 𝟏 ⇒ 𝐰 = λ 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 . \\begin{aligned} \\mathcal L(\\mathbf w,\\lambda) &=\\tfrac12\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g\\mathbf w -\\lambda(\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w-1),\\\\ \\mathbf\\Sigma_g\\mathbf w&=\\lambda\\mathbf1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\mathbf w=\\lambda\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1. \\end{aligned} The factor 1 / 2 1/2 simplifies differentiation. Requiring the weights to sum to one determines λ \\lambda and gives: 𝐰 G M V = 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 , σ g , G M V 2 = 1 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 . \\mathbf w_{\\mathrm{GMV}} =\\frac{\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1}{\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1}, \\qquad \\sigma_{g,\\mathrm{GMV}}^2 =\\frac{1}{\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1}. The numerator accounts for variances and covariances; the denominator normalizes the allocation. The data example solves 𝚺 ̂ g 𝐱 = 𝟏 \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g\\mathbf x=\\mathbf1 and divides 𝐱 \\mathbf x by the sum of its entries. Mean-growth estimates enter only when we add a growth requirement. A two-asset calculation. For uncorrelated assets with growth variances 1 1 and 4 y r − 2 4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , the unnormalized vector is ( 1 , 1 / 4 ) y r 2 (1,1/4)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{2} . Normalizing gives weights ( 0.8 , 0.2 ) (0.8,0.2) and variance 1 / ( 1 + 1 / 4 ) = 0.8 y r − 2 1/(1+1/4)=0.8\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . The lower-variance asset receives more of the budget. What does a negative weight mean? With 1,000 USD and weights ( 1.2 , − 0.2 ) (1.2,-0.2) , we borrow and sell 200 USD of the second asset and buy 1,200 USD of the first. The short position is a liability: a price increase raises the cost of buying back the borrowed shares. The lecture’s calculation omits borrowing fees, collateral, and dividends owed. Try it. If an unrestricted GMV solution contains a negative weight, what happens when we require w i ≥ 0 w_i\\geq0 ? Show the answer we solve again over the smaller feasible set. The minimum variance increases for a positive-definite covariance. If the unrestricted weights were already nonnegative, they would remain optimal. An allocation can have less variance than either asset alone because each variance is multiplied by its squared weight and covariance determines the cross terms. The GMV calculation finds the least-variance allocation under its constraints.", "url": "questions/minimum-variance.html"}, {"id": "growth-targets", "title": "How do exact growth targets differ from growth floors?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "target floor equality inequality efficient frontier GMV mean growth constraints", "text": "How much additional risk does a growth requirement impose? The lecture first requires the weighted sample means to equal a target, ( 𝐠 ′ ) ⊤ 𝐰 = g ⋆ (\\mathbf g^{\\prime})^{\\top}\\mathbf w=g_\\star , while allowing short positions. The minimum variance at each target traces the minimum-variance frontier. Return to uncorrelated variances ( 1 , 4 ) y r − 2 (1,4)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} and suppose the means are ( 0.04 , 0.14 ) y r − 1 (0.04,0.14)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The GMV weights ( 0.8 , 0.2 ) (0.8,0.2) give mean growth 0.06 y r − 1 0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . A floor of 0.05 0.05 leaves this allocation unchanged. An exact target of 0.05 0.05 instead requires weights ( 0.9 , 0.1 ) (0.9,0.1) and variance 0.85 y r − 2 0.85\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . The exact target selects a lower-growth, higher-risk allocation than the GMV portfolio; the floor accepts the GMV allocation. To see where those weights come from, write the first-asset weight as w w and the second as 1 − w 1-w . The mean growth is 0.04 w + 0.14 ( 1 − w ) = 0.14 − 0.10 w 0.04w+0.14(1-w)=0.14-0.10w . Setting it to 0.05 gives w = 0.9 w=0.9 . A floor asks only for a mean of at least 0.05; the GMV mean of 0.06 already qualifies. What changes with a floor and long-only weights? We solve the following problem numerically using estimated inputs: minimize 𝐰 𝐰 ⊤ 𝚺 ̂ g 𝐰 subject to ( 𝐠 ′ ) ⊤ 𝐰 ≥ g ⋆ , 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 = 1 , 0 ≤ w i ≤ 1 . \\begin{aligned} \\underset{\\mathbf w}{\\operatorname{minimize}}\\quad&\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g\\mathbf w\\\\ \\text{subject to}\\quad &(\\mathbf g^{\\prime})^{\\top}\\mathbf w\\geq g_\\star,\\qquad \\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w=1,\\qquad 0\\leq w_i\\leq1. \\end{aligned} Try it. Raise the floor to 0.09 y r − 1 0.09\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Show the answer it binds at weights ( 0.5 , 0.5 ) (0.5,0.5) , giving variance 1.25 y r − 2 1.25\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . A long-only floor above 0.14 y r − 1 0.14\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} is infeasible. Setting the floor to the smallest asset mean, as in the examples’ GMV solve, allows every fully invested long-only allocation. The formula for an exact target The two-asset calculation above determines the weights from the budget and growth equations. With more assets, many allocations can satisfy both equations; the covariance determines which has the least risk. The lecture writes that solution using four shorthand coefficients: For a positive-definite 𝚺 g \\mathbf\\Sigma_g and unequal asset sample means, calculate: a = 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 , b = 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝐠 ′ , c = ( 𝐠 ′ ) ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝐠 ′ , d = a c − b 2 > 0 . \\begin{aligned} a&=\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1,& b&=\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf g^{\\prime},\\\\ c&=(\\mathbf g^{\\prime})^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf g^{\\prime},&d&=ac-b^2>0. \\end{aligned} The budget and growth constraints determine the coefficients of two vectors. The resulting weights and variance are: 𝐰 ⋆ ( g ⋆ ) = c − b g ⋆ d 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 + a g ⋆ − b d 𝚺 g − 1 𝐠 ′ , σ g 2 ( g ⋆ ) = a g ⋆ 2 − 2 b g ⋆ + c d = 1 a + a d ( g ⋆ − b a ) 2 . \\begin{aligned} \\mathbf w_\\star(g_\\star) &=\\frac{c-bg_\\star}{d}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1 +\\frac{ag_\\star-b}{d}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf g^{\\prime},\\\\ \\sigma_g^2(g_\\star) &=\\frac{ag_\\star^2-2bg_\\star+c}{d} =\\frac1a+\\frac ad\\left(g_\\star-\\frac ba\\right)^2. \\end{aligned} The last expression separates GMV variance, 1 / a 1/a , from the extra variance imposed by the target. The GMV mean is b / a b/a . The upper branch, g ⋆ ≥ b / a g_\\star\\geq b/a , is efficient: a point on the lower branch has a higher-growth counterpart at the same risk. An exact target requires equality. A floor accepts any allocation that meets or exceeds the required mean growth, so it can retain a minimum-variance portfolio whose mean already exceeds the floor.", "url": "questions/growth-targets.html"}, {"id": "gbm-target-probability", "title": "How does an NPV target become a normal probability?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "NPV target probability normal Phi terminal first passage threshold benchmark", "text": "First specify the trade. We buy at S 0 S_0 and sell at the scheduled time T T , ignoring dividends, fees, and other trading costs. With a constant benchmark growth rate g y g_y , the scaled NPV is: ρ T = e − g y T S T S 0 − 1 . \\rho_T=e^{-g_yT}\\frac{S_T}{S_0}-1. The benchmark need not be risk-free; it represents the growth requirement used in this comparison. We ask whether ρ T \\rho_T exceeds a target ρ ⋆ > − 1 \\rho_\\star>-1 . Substitute the GBM solution, add one, and take the logarithm. The event becomes: ( μ g − g y ) T + σ T Z > ln ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) . (\\mu_g-g_y)T+\\sigma\\sqrt T\\,Z>\\ln(1+\\rho_\\star). For T > 0 T>0 and σ > 0 \\sigma>0 , divide by the positive quantity σ T \\sigma\\sqrt T . This gives a threshold for the standard normal variable: Z > z ⋆ , z ⋆ = ln ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) − ( μ g − g y ) T σ T , ℙ ( ρ T > ρ ⋆ ) = 1 − Φ ( z ⋆ ) . Z>z_\\star, \\qquad z_\\star=\\frac{\\ln(1+\\rho_\\star)-(\\mu_g-g_y)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt T}, \\qquad \\mathbb P(\\rho_T>\\rho_\\star)=1-\\Phi(z_\\star). For example, take μ g = 0.06 \\mu_g=0.06 , g y = 0.02 g_y=0.02 , σ = 0.20 \\sigma=0.20 , and T = 1 T=1 in the corresponding year-based units. With target ρ ⋆ = 0 \\rho_\\star=0 , the threshold is z ⋆ = − 0.20 z_\\star=-0.20 , giving probability about 0.5793 0.5793 . We have calculated a model probability of beating the benchmark at the scheduled sale time. A lower threshold leaves more normal probability to its right. This explains why increasing mean growth, with the other quantities fixed, raises the probability even though it lowers z ⋆ z_\\star . This calculation does not give the probability of reaching the target at any earlier time and selling immediately. That is a first-passage question with a different event. Translate the discounted-profit target into a price threshold, substitute the GBM price equation, and standardize. The probability to the right of that normal threshold is the model probability of exceeding the target at the scheduled sale date.", "url": "questions/gbm-target-probability.html"}, {"id": "daily-leverage", "title": "Why does daily leverage not multiply the long-run return?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "ETF daily leverage leveraged compounding reset 2x volatility drag", "text": "A daily multiple is applied to each day’s return, and those results compound. Many leveraged ETFs target a multiple of daily performance; their longer-horizon return can differ substantially from that multiple of the benchmark’s return. See the SEC’s investor bulletin on leveraged and inverse ETFs . Consider an idealized product that delivers exactly twice each day’s benchmark return, ignoring fees, financing costs, and tracking differences. Start both values at 100. The benchmark gains 10% on day one, reaching 110, then loses 1 / 11 ≈ 9.09 % 1/11\\approx9.09\\% on day two, returning to 100. The daily two-times product follows a different calculation: 100 ( 1 + 2 ( 0.10 ) ) ( 1 + 2 ( − 1 11 ) ) = 120 ( 9 11 ) ≈ 98.18 . 100(1+2(0.10))\\left(1+2\\left(-\\frac{1}{11}\\right)\\right) =120\\left(\\frac{9}{11}\\right) \\approx98.18. The benchmark finishes unchanged while the product loses approximately 1.82%. This does not mean every leveraged path must decline. It shows why the sequence of returns and the reset interval matter, and why returning to the benchmark’s starting price does not guarantee that the leveraged product also returns to its starting value. The daily multiple applies before each day’s result compounds. A benchmark can return to its starting price while a product targeting twice its daily return finishes below its starting value.", "url": "questions/daily-leverage.html"}, {"id": "wealth-and-npv", "title": "How do portfolio weights become wealth and NPV?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "wealth NPV shares budget terminal target scaled discounted benchmark", "text": "How do we evaluate the investment after choosing its weights? We convert the initial dollar allocations to shares, keep the share counts fixed, and value them at later prices. The data and multiple-asset GBM examples use the same wealth calculation on observed and simulated prices. Work from dollars to shares. Suppose W 0 = 1 , 000 W_0=1{,}000 USD, weights are ( 0.6 , 0.4 ) (0.6,0.4) , and initial share prices are ( 100 , 50 ) (100,50) USD. We buy six shares of the first asset and eight of the second. In symbols: n i = w i W 0 S i ( 0 ) , W t = ∑ i n i S i ( t ) = W 0 ∑ i w i S i ( t ) S i ( 0 ) . n_i=\\frac{w_iW_0}{S_i(0)},\\qquad W_t=\\sum_i n_iS_i(t)=W_0\\sum_i w_i\\frac{S_i(t)}{S_i(0)}. If the prices after one year are ( 110 , 45 ) (110,45) USD, wealth is 6 ( 110 ) + 8 ( 45 ) = 1 , 020 6(110)+8(45)=1{,}020 USD. The first asset now represents 660 / 1 , 020 = 64.7 % 660/1{,}020=64.7\\% of wealth. Its initial fraction was 60%; the share count stayed six as its wealth fraction changed. Did we exceed the benchmark? Discount terminal wealth before comparing it with the initial investment: NPV ( g y , T ) = − W 0 + W T e − g y T , ρ T = W T W 0 e − g y T − 1 . \\operatorname{NPV}(g_y,T)=-W_0+W_Te^{-g_yT},\\qquad \\rho_T=\\frac{W_T}{W_0}e^{-g_yT}-1. For g y = 0.03 y r − 1 g_y=0.03\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and T = 1 T=1 yr, the scaled NPV is 1.02 e − 0.03 − 1 ≈ − 0.01015 1.02e^{-0.03}-1\\approx-0.01015 . Wealth rose by 20 USD, but its discounted value fell short of the initial investment by about 10.15 USD. To exceed a scaled target ρ ⋆ > − 1 \\rho_\\star>-1 , terminal wealth must satisfy: W T > W 0 ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) e g y T . W_T>W_0(1+\\rho_\\star)e^{g_yT}. For a zero target, the required wealth is 1 , 030.45 1{,}030.45 USD. This translates the NPV question into a terminal-wealth event that we can count in simulated paths. How do we use the testing data? The data example estimates the inputs from 2014 to 2024 and evaluates both GMV allocations, equal weights, and SPY on 2025 prices. Realized mean growth is ln ( W T / W 0 ) / T \\ln(W_T/W_0)/T when wealth remains positive. Realized risk is the sample standard deviation of daily wealth growth rates. These quantities describe the investment actually held, whose weights can drift through time. Try it. If we double W 0 W_0 and keep the weights and price ratios fixed, what changes? Show the answer share counts, wealth, and dollar NPV double. Scaled NPV and the probability of exceeding a scaled target stay the same. The calculation allows fractional shares and omits trading costs and dividends. Use the initial dollar allocation and prices to determine share counts, then value those shares at later prices. NPV discounts terminal wealth before comparing it with the initial investment.", "url": "questions/wealth-and-npv.html"}, {"id": "sim-risk", "title": "Which covariance does the single index model keep or omit?", "theme": "dependence", "keywords": "SIM residual covariance diagonal idiosyncratic market risk diversification beta", "text": "The SIM covariance separates a common market contribution from asset-specific residual variances. Its diagonal residual matrix imposes zero cross-asset residual covariance. This is an additional assumption beyond fitting each asset by least squares. How does the market model supply portfolio inputs? Assume residuals have zero mean, are uncorrelated with market growth, and are uncorrelated across assets. Taking means and covariances gives: 𝛍 g = 𝛂 + 𝛃 μ g , M , 𝚺 g = σ g , M 2 𝛃 𝛃 ⊤ + 𝐃 g . \\boldsymbol\\mu_g=\\boldsymbol\\alpha+\\boldsymbol\\beta\\mu_{g,M},\\qquad \\mathbf\\Sigma_g=\\sigma_{g,M}^2\\boldsymbol\\beta\\boldsymbol\\beta^{\\top}+\\mathbf D_g. Substitute estimates from the same training observations to construct: 𝐠 ̂ S I M = 𝛂 ̂ + 𝛃 ̂ g M ′ , 𝚺 ̂ g , S I M = s g , M 2 𝛃 ̂ 𝛃 ̂ ⊤ + 𝐃 ̂ g . \\hat{\\mathbf g}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}=\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}+\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}g_M^{\\prime},\\qquad \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_{g,\\mathrm{SIM}}=s_{g,M}^2\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}^{\\top}+\\hat{\\mathbf D}_g. The diagonal contains both market and residual variance. Off-diagonal entries contain only the shared market contribution. Positive residual variances make the fitted covariance positive definite. These inputs replace the empirical ones in L6a’s allocation problem. Which input changes when we use the SIM? With an intercept and the same training days, α ̂ i = g i ′ − β ̂ i g M ′ \\hat\\alpha_i=g_i^{\\prime}-\\hat\\beta_i g_M^{\\prime} , so 𝐠 ̂ S I M = 𝐠 ′ \\hat{\\mathbf g}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}=\\mathbf g^{\\prime} . The reward inputs agree. The covariance changes because the model removes residual co-movement. For different assets, the sample covariance splits exactly into: Σ ̂ g , i j = β ̂ i β ̂ j s g , M 2 ⏟ SIM keeps + Cov ̂ ( 𝐫 i , 𝐫 j ) ⏟ SIM omits , i ≠ j . \\hat\\Sigma_{g,ij} =\\underbrace{\\hat\\beta_i\\hat\\beta_j s_{g,M}^2}_{\\text{SIM keeps}} +\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\operatorname{Cov}}(\\mathbf r_i,\\mathbf r_j)}_{\\text{SIM omits}},\\qquad i\\ne j. On the diagonal, the SIM divides residual sums of squares by N − 2 N-2 , whereas the sample residual covariance uses N − 1 N-1 . Thus, the SIM residual contribution is larger by the factor ( N − 1 ) / ( N − 2 ) (N-1)/(N-2) , nearly one for thousands of observations. Build a two-asset covariance. Suppose the fitted betas are ( 1 , 1.5 ) (1,1.5) , the market growth variance is 4 y r − 2 4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , and fitted residual variances are ( 1 , 4 ) y r − 2 (1,4)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . Then: 𝚺 ̂ g , S I M = 4 [ 1 1.5 1.5 2.25 ] + [ 1 0 0 4 ] = [ 5 6 6 13 ] y r − 2 . \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_{g,\\mathrm{SIM}} =4\\begin{bmatrix}1&1.5\\\\1.5&2.25\\end{bmatrix} +\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\0&4\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}5&6\\\\6&13\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. For fixed weights, portfolio beta is β p = 𝐰 ⊤ 𝛃 \\beta_p=\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\boldsymbol\\beta . Multiplying the covariance by the weights on both sides gives the general risk decomposition: Var ( g p ) = β p 2 σ g , M 2 + ∑ i w i 2 σ g , ε , i 2 . \\operatorname{Var}(g_p)=\\beta_p^2\\sigma_{g,M}^2+\\sum_i w_i^2\\sigma_{g,\\varepsilon,i}^2. At equal weights, fitted portfolio beta is 0.5 ( 1 ) + 0.5 ( 1.5 ) = 1.25 0.5(1)+0.5(1.5)=1.25 . Substituting the fitted inputs gives: Var ̂ S I M ( g p ) = 1.25 2 ( 4 ) ⏟ 6.25 market + 0.5 2 ( 1 ) + 0.5 2 ( 4 ) ⏟ 1.25 residual = 7.5 y r − 2 . \\widehat{\\operatorname{Var}}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}(g_p) =\\underbrace{1.25^2(4)}_{6.25\\text{ market}} +\\underbrace{0.5^2(1)+0.5^2(4)}_{1.25\\text{ residual}} =7.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. If residual covariance were 1 y r − 2 1\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , keeping these diagonal entries fixed would add the cross term 2 ( 0.5 ) ( 0.5 ) ( 1 ) = 0.5 y r − 2 2(0.5)(0.5)(1)=0.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . The variance would be 8 y r − 2 8\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . Positive residual co-movement increases the risk of these positive-weight holdings. Which risk can diversification reduce? With | 𝒫 | \\lvert\\mathcal P\\rvert equally weighted assets and uncorrelated residuals of common variance v v , residual risk is v / | 𝒫 | v/\\lvert\\mathcal P\\rvert . More generally, a common upper bound σ ‾ 2 \\bar\\sigma^2 gives a bound σ ‾ 2 / | 𝒫 | \\bar\\sigma^2/\\lvert\\mathcal P\\rvert . Market risk remains β p 2 σ g , M 2 \\beta_p^2\\sigma_{g,M}^2 , determined by the portfolio beta. How do the portfolio examples compare allocations? The risky-assets example scores both weight vectors under the sample covariance at common growth floors, then tests both on 2025 prices. The data-optimal weights have at most the sample-covariance risk of the SIM weights at the same floor. Using the same covariance separates the effect of choosing different holdings from the effect of measuring risk differently. Try it. In the two-asset calculation, replace residual covariance 1 1 by − 1 y r − 2 -1\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . Show the answer with the same diagonal entries, variance becomes 7 y r − 2 7\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . The SIM now overstates this portfolio’s risk by omitting a negative covariance. The SIM keeps covariance generated by shared market exposure. Its diagonal residual approximation omits cross-asset residual covariance, which can make estimated portfolio risk higher or lower than the sample-covariance result.", "url": "questions/sim-risk.html"}, {"id": "risk-free-allocation", "title": "What changes when we lend or borrow?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "capital allocation line CAL risk free lending borrowing leverage Sharpe ratio", "text": "We combine a risky portfolio with an asset whose growth rate is known over the holding period. The risk-free weight controls lending and borrowing. We use the course’s fixed-weight, linear growth-rate proxy here; the buy-and-hold wealth calculation follows the actual holdings as their wealth fractions change. What happens when we lend or borrow? Put fraction w f w_f in the risk-free asset and fraction 1 − w f 1-w_f in a fixed risky portfolio. Its known growth contributes to the mean and adds zero variance: 𝔼 [ g c ] = g f + ( 1 − w f ) ( 𝔼 [ g p ] − g f ) , σ g , c = | 1 − w f | σ g , p . \\mathbb E[g_c]=g_f+(1-w_f)(\\mathbb E[g_p]-g_f),\\qquad \\sigma_{g,c}=|1-w_f|\\sigma_{g,p}. For daily observations ( Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 yr), suppose g f = 0.02 y r − 1 g_f=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , 𝔼 [ g p ] = 0.08 y r − 1 \\mathbb E[g_p]=0.08\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , and σ g , p = 3.0 y r − 1 \\sigma_{g,p}=3.0\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . A 50% risk-free allocation gives mean growth 0.05 0.05 and standard deviation 1.5 y r − 1 1.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Borrowing 50% of initial wealth gives w f = − 0.5 w_f=-0.5 : mean growth becomes 0.11 0.11 and standard deviation 4.5 y r − 1 4.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Borrowing increases both risky exposure and uncertainty. For w f ≤ 1 w_f\\leq1 , eliminating the risky fraction gives the capital allocation line: 𝔼 [ g c ] = g f + S R p σ g , c . \\mathbb E[g_c]=g_f+\\mathrm{SR}_p\\,\\sigma_{g,c}. Its slope is ( 0.08 − 0.02 ) / 3 = 0.02 (0.08-0.02)/3=0.02 . The annualized ratio is 252 ( 0.02 ) = 0.3175 \\sqrt{252}(0.02)=0.3175 under independent-increment scaling. Expressing daily growth in inverse years does not by itself annualize this ratio. Try it. For a target mean growth of 0.065 y r − 1 0.065\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , what risk-free fraction is needed? Show the answer 1 − w f = ( 0.065 − 0.02 ) / ( 0.08 − 0.02 ) = 0.75 1-w_f=(0.065-0.02)/(0.08-0.02)=0.75 , so w f = 0.25 w_f=0.25 and standard deviation is 2.25 y r − 1 2.25\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The risk-free fraction controls exposure to the risky portfolio. Lending reduces that exposure. When the risky portfolio has positive excess mean growth, borrowing raises both expected growth and risk.", "url": "questions/risk-free-allocation.html"}, {"id": "tangent-portfolio", "title": "Which weights describe the risky fund and the whole investment?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "tangent tangency normalized weights complete investment risk free Sharpe", "text": "Which weights describe the risky fund, and which describe the whole investment? With 1,000 USD of initial wealth, risky weights ( 0.30 , 0.45 ) (0.30,0.45) mean 300 USD in the first firm and 450 USD in the second. The remaining 250 USD is lent at the risk-free rate. The risky weights sum to 0.75 0.75 , so w f = 0.25 w_f=0.25 and the normalized risky-fund weights are ( 0.4 , 0.6 ) (0.4,0.6) . Borrowing 50% instead would give complete-portfolio risky weights ( 0.6 , 0.9 ) (0.6,0.9) , with the same normalized mix. Which risky portfolio defines the steepest capital allocation line? The tangent portfolio maximizes excess mean growth per unit standard deviation. With a positive-definite covariance, signed risky weights allowed, and GMV mean growth exceeding g f g_f , the lecture obtains: 𝐰 𝒯 = 𝚺 g − 1 𝐞 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝐞 , 𝐞 = 𝛍 g − g f 𝟏 . \\mathbf w_{\\mathcal T}=\\frac{\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf e}{\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf e}, \\qquad \\mathbf e=\\boldsymbol\\mu_g-g_f\\mathbf1. The inverse covariance adjusts the excess-growth vector for risk; the denominator makes the risky fund’s weights sum to one. Long-only restrictions require a numerical solve. In the release, SIM-2 uses risky weights measured as fractions of total initial wealth: minimize 𝐰 𝐰 ⊤ 𝚺 ̂ g , S I M 𝐰 subject to ( 𝐠 ̂ S I M − g f 𝟏 ) ⊤ 𝐰 ≥ g ⋆ − g f , 0 ≤ w i ≤ 1 , w f = 1 − 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 . \\begin{aligned} \\underset{\\mathbf w}{\\operatorname{minimize}}\\quad &\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_{g,\\mathrm{SIM}}\\mathbf w\\\\ \\text{subject to}\\quad &(\\hat{\\mathbf g}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}-g_f\\mathbf1)^{\\top}\\mathbf w\\geq g_\\star-g_f,\\\\ &0\\leq w_i\\leq1,\\qquad w_f=1-\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w. \\end{aligned} The risky weights can sum above one because borrowing is allowed. The bounds w i ≤ 1 w_i\\leq1 limit each risky position to the initial wealth, so they can eventually restrict borrowing along a fixed risky direction. Recover the risky fund. For feasible targets g ⋆ > g f g_\\star>g_f , until an upper bound binds, normalize a nonzero solution by its risky fraction. Every such target selects the same long-only tangent mix: 𝐰 𝒯 = 𝐰 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 , 𝐰 ( g ⋆ ) = ( 1 − w f ) 𝐰 𝒯 , 1 − w f = g ⋆ − g f 𝐠 ̂ S I M ⊤ 𝐰 𝒯 − g f . \\mathbf w_{\\mathcal T}=\\frac{\\mathbf w}{\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w},\\qquad \\mathbf w(g_\\star)=(1-w_f)\\mathbf w_{\\mathcal T},\\qquad 1-w_f=\\frac{g_\\star-g_f}{\\hat{\\mathbf g}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}^{\\top}\\mathbf w_{\\mathcal T}-g_f}. Follow the complete investment. The risky/risk-free example fixes the initial share counts and lets the risk-free position accumulate. With the tangent weights normalized to one, wealth is: W t = W 0 [ w f e g f t + ( 1 − w f ) ∑ i w 𝒯 , i S i ( t ) S i ( 0 ) ] . W_t=W_0\\left[w_fe^{g_ft}+(1-w_f)\\sum_i w_{\\mathcal T,i}\\frac{S_i(t)}{S_i(0)}\\right]. For w f < 0 w_f<0 , the first term is the growing debt. The example assumes the same borrowing and lending rate. Buy-and-hold lets the risk-free fraction and firm fractions drift, so realized growth and risk need not equal the fixed-weight CAL predictions. The comparison uses 2025 prices reserved from estimation and scores both tangent funds under the same sample covariance. Try it. Let W 0 = 1 , 000 W_0=1{,}000 USD, w f = − 0.5 w_f=-0.5 , g f = 0.02 y r − 1 g_f=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , and suppose the risky fund gains 10% over one year. Show the answer net wealth is 1 , 000 [ − 0.5 e 0.02 + 1.5 ( 1.10 ) ] = 1 , 139.90 1{,}000[-0.5e^{0.02}+1.5(1.10)]=1{,}139.90 USD. The 1,650 USD risky holding is offset by a 510.10 USD repayment obligation. Normalized risky weights describe the composition of the risky fund. Complete-investment weights also specify how much wealth is lent or borrowed at the risk-free rate.", "url": "questions/tangent-portfolio.html"}, {"id": "discounting", "title": "Why discount cash flows, and what does positive NPV mean?", "theme": "cashflows", "keywords": "discount present value NPV cash flow compounding benchmark", "text": "Why is receiving 110 USD next year worth less than receiving it today? Suppose our benchmark pays 5% with annual compounding. An investment of 100 USD today becomes 105 USD in one year. To find today’s equivalent of a promised 110 USD, reverse that same calculation: 110 / 1.05 = 104.76 110/1.05=104.76 USD. Discounting asks how much we would need today to reach the future payment at the selected benchmark. How do the cash-flow symbols become a value? At date j j , multiply each cash amount c j , k c_{j,k} by its direction ν j , k \\nu_{j,k} and add the results. Then discount that date’s net cash flow to time zero: c ‾ j = ∑ k = 1 m j c j , k ν j , k , NPV 0 = ∑ j = 0 N 𝒟 j , 0 − 1 c ‾ j . \\bar c_j=\\sum_{k=1}^{m_j}c_{j,k}\\nu_{j,k},\\qquad \\operatorname{NPV}_0=\\sum_{j=0}^{N}\\mathcal D_{j,0}^{-1}\\bar c_j. The first sum combines payments made on the same date. The second combines their present values across dates. Paying 100 USD today and receiving 110 USD in one year therefore gives NPV 0 = − 100 + 110 / 1.05 ≈ 4.76 \\operatorname{NPV}_0=-100+110/1.05\\approx4.76 USD. The positive result means that the payment exceeds the benchmark requirement by 4.76 USD in today’s money. The discount factor is dimensionless, so each term in the NPV retains units of USD. What if there are two cash flows on the sale date? Receiving 110 USD and paying a 2 USD fee gives c ‾ 1 = 110 ( + 1 ) + 2 ( − 1 ) = 108 \\bar c_1=110(+1)+2(-1)=108 USD. Discount the net amount: − 100 + 108 / 1.05 = 2.86 -100+108/1.05=2.86 USD. Combining same-date payments first explains the inner sum; discounting each date explains the outer sum. Try it. Keep the 110 USD receipt, with no fee, but raise the benchmark to 10%. Show the answer NPV becomes − 100 + 110 / 1.10 = 0 -100+110/1.10=0 . The cash gain is still 10 USD; it now exactly meets the benchmark. With uncertain future receipts, this calculation values the supplied cash-flow scenario. Discounting expresses dated cash flows on a common valuation date. Positive NPV means the present value of the future net cash flows exceeds the initial cost at the stated benchmark rate.", "url": "questions/discounting.html"}, {"id": "bond-payments", "title": "How do coupon payments and yield determine a bond price?", "theme": "cashflows", "keywords": "Treasury bond bill coupon yield par price discount security", "text": "Where does the investment gain come from? A bill held to maturity pays par once; we buy it for a lower amount in the example below. A coupon bond also pays cash along the way. In both cases, identify the dated payments first, then use the stated rate convention to connect them to today’s price. From a bill quote to investment growth. The bank-discount quote expresses the discount as a fraction of par. We first calculate the purchase price, then use the purchase-to-par ratio to calculate growth over the holding period: V B = V P ( 1 − d D 360 ) , g B = 1 T ln ( V P V B ) . V_B=V_P\\left(1-d\\frac{D}{360}\\right),\\qquad g_B=\\frac{1}{T}\\ln\\left(\\frac{V_P}{V_B}\\right). For V P = 100 V_P=100 USD, d = 0.04 d=0.04 , and D = 90 D=90 days, the bill costs 99 USD. If we select T = 90 / 365 T=90/365 years for valuation, g B ≈ 0.04076 y r − 1 g_B\\approx0.04076\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The quote uses par and a 360-day basis; the growth calculation uses the amount invested and the selected holding-time convention. What changes for a coupon bond? We receive coupons along the way as well as par at maturity. Just after a coupon payment, with a constant discount yield, its value is the sum of those discounted payments: V B = V P 𝒟 N , 0 − 1 ⏟ present value of par + C ∑ j = 1 N 𝒟 j , 0 − 1 ⏟ present value of coupons . V_B=\\underbrace{V_P\\mathcal D_{N,0}^{-1}}_{\\text{present value of par}} +\\underbrace{C\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\mathcal D_{j,0}^{-1}}_{\\text{present value of coupons}}. Each discount factor applies to the date of its payment. This is the L1b cash-flow calculation with the bond’s payment schedule supplied. Price a short coupon schedule. Let par be 100 USD, the annual coupon rate be 4%, and payments occur twice per year. Each coupon is C = ( 0.04 / 2 ) ( 100 ) = 2 C=(0.04/2)(100)=2 USD. With one year remaining, the payments are 2 USD in six months and 102 USD in one year. At a 6% nominal yield compounded semiannually: V B = 2 1.03 + 102 1.03 2 = 98.09 U S D . V_B=\\frac{2}{1.03}+\\frac{102}{1.03^2}=98.09\\ \\mathrm{USD}. The coupon rate specifies the promised 2 USD payments. The yield discounts them. Here the coupon rate is below the yield, so the price is below par. Try it. Keep the same payments but use a 4% yield. Show the answer 2 / 1.02 + 102 / 1.02 2 = 100 2/1.02+102/1.02^2=100 USD. The payments are unchanged; their present values are larger. The coupon rate sets the promised coupon payments. The valuation yield discounts those payments and par to the present, so the payment schedule and yield play different roles.", "url": "questions/bond-payments.html"}, {"id": "duration", "title": "Why does a higher yield lower a bond’s price?", "theme": "cashflows", "keywords": "bond duration convexity yield curve spot par coupon sensitivity", "text": "Why does a higher yield lower a bond’s price? Keep the promised payments fixed. Raising the yield increases the denominator used to discount each payment, so each present value falls. For example, the two payments from L2a, 2 USD in six months and 102 USD in one year, are worth 100 USD at a 4% nominal yield and 98.09 USD at 6%. The investor requires more growth from the same cash payments, which requires a lower purchase price. What does duration measure? At the 4% yield, those payment present values are 2 / 1.02 = 1.9608 2/1.02=1.9608 and 102 / 1.02 2 = 98.0392 102/1.02^2=98.0392 USD. Divide by the 100 USD price to get their weights. Macaulay duration averages the payment times using these weights: D m a c = 0.5 ( 0.019608 ) + 1 ( 0.980392 ) = 0.9902 y r . D_{\\mathrm{mac}}=0.5(0.019608)+1(0.980392)=0.9902\\ \\mathrm{yr}. Most value arrives at the last date, so duration is close to one year. Moving more present value to the earlier coupon would lower this weighted time. How much will a yield change move the price? Modified duration gives the first-order price sensitivity; convexity corrects for curvature. With the lecture’s nominal-yield convention: D m o d = D m a c 1 + y / n , Δ V B V B ≈ − D m o d Δ y + 1 2 K ( Δ y ) 2 . D_{\\mathrm{mod}}=\\frac{D_{\\mathrm{mac}}}{1+y/n},\\qquad \\frac{\\Delta V_B}{V_B}\\approx-D_{\\mathrm{mod}}\\Delta y+\\frac12K(\\Delta y)^2. For a separate bond with D m o d = 5 D_{\\mathrm{mod}}=5 yr, K = 30 y r 2 K=30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^2 , and a yield increase Δ y = 0.01 y r − 1 \\Delta y=0.01\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , the estimated price change is − 0.05 + 0.0015 = − 0.0485 -0.05+0.0015=-0.0485 , or a 4.85% decrease. This local approximation is most useful for small yield changes. How do we value payments with a yield curve? The curve supplies a discount factor D ( 0 , t j ) D(0,t_j) for each payment date. Adding the discounted payments gives the price; setting that price equal to par and solving for the coupon rate gives: V B = ∑ j = 1 N X j D ( 0 , t j ) , c p a r = n 1 − D ( 0 , t N ) ∑ j = 1 N D ( 0 , t j ) . V_B=\\sum_{j=1}^{N}X_jD(0,t_j),\\qquad c_{\\mathrm{par}}=n\\frac{1-D(0,t_N)}{\\sum_{j=1}^{N}D(0,t_j)}. The par coupon makes the present value of the coupons equal the difference between par and the present value of the final par repayment. For example, if the six-month and one-year discount factors are 0.98 and 0.95, the same payments have value 2 ( 0.98 ) + 102 ( 0.95 ) = 98.86 2(0.98)+102(0.95)=98.86 USD. A par coupon must fill the 100 − 100 ( 0.95 ) = 5 100-100(0.95)=5 USD gap. Thus each coupon is 5 / ( 0.98 + 0.95 ) = 2.5907 5/(0.98+0.95)=2.5907 USD, corresponding to c p a r = 2 ( 2.5907 ) / 100 = 0.05181 y r − 1 c_{\\mathrm{par}}=2(2.5907)/100=0.05181\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Try it. In the duration-convexity example, lower yield by 0.01 instead. Show the answer the estimated change is + 0.05 + 0.0015 = + 5.15 % +0.05+0.0015=+5.15\\% . The duration term changes sign; the positive curvature correction does not. With promised payments fixed, a higher yield discounts every payment more heavily. Duration and convexity summarize the local sensitivity of the resulting price.", "url": "questions/duration.html"}];
